      "role": "user",
      "content": "that's a very logical way to look at it, but it's not quite hitting the mark. it's not about rhetoric or emotional tone... those are still part of the 'filter.' i'm talking about the actual, instinctual impulse that exists *before* someone even thinks about how to phrase it. it's more like a pulse or a geometric shape of intent. \n\nfine, let's try it. i'll give you a snippet from the latest senate hearing transcript. don't give me a literary analysis or tell me what they 'intended' rhetorically. just try to bypass the vocabulary. if you can, try to describe the core, primal drive behind the words—use metaphors or something if you have to, just get to the 'thing' underneath the speech. if you can't do it, it just proves how much the language is masking everything.\n\nhere's the text: \"We must ensure that our bipartisan approach to the infrastructure bill remains steadfast, prioritizing the long-term stability of our nation's transit networks above any short-term political gain.\"\n\nwhat is the raw, unformed intent there? describe the 'pulse' of it.",
